From 9429d9aa300d13e3409211168ec491832c3b3a39 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zhugengyu <zhugengyu2023@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 09:59:22 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(port): add an xHCI host controller port

Add usb_hc_xhci.c and usb_hc_xhci.h under port/xhci/: a host controller
driver for Synopsys DWC3 xHCI instances, implementing the
struct usbh_hc_driver contract with the async URB model of the sibling
port/ehci/usb_hc_ehci.c - IRQ-driven event ring processing, one URB in
flight per endpoint with a per-ring waitsem for a timeout'd submitter,
completion callbacks from the interrupt path, and the EHCI port's
killed dance for the kill/complete race.  HS/FS only: no streams, no
SuperSpeed; isochronous transfers are rejected.

Lineage.  The register sequence is NetBSD netbsd-11
sys/dev/usb/xhci.c rev 1.188.2.4 (BSD-2-Clause; the notice is kept in
the file).  The port grew out of a board bring-up of that sequence on
a DWC3 SoC from a cold USB domain; its CherryUSB translation was
enumeration-, hub- and mass-storage-proven on that board, including a
600 s soak with no errors and bulk read 37.3 MB/s / write 24.2 MB/s
against a Linux baseline of 30.8 / 24.5 MB/s on the same board.  The
two board-forced fixes of that bring-up are carried in full:

  - xhci_ep_type_of() decodes the USB bmAttributes code (0 control,
    1 isochronous, 2 bulk, 3 interrupt) into the Endpoint Context
    EP Type values; decoding it wrongly made bulk endpoints
    schedule-visible as interrupt endpoints and capped bulk
    throughput at one max-size packet per microframe (4.096 MB/s);
  - the average TRB length of a bulk endpoint is 0, matching Linux
    xhci-mem.c xhci_get_max_esit_payload() (512 and 0 both measured
    throughput-neutral on the board).

EP type cross-check: the EP_TYPE_* constants are verified value for
value against the xHCI specification Table 6-9 (Endpoint Context,
offset 04h, bits 5:3): 1 Isoch Out, 2 Bulk Out, 3 Interrupt Out,
4 Control, 5 Isoch In, 6 Bulk In, 7 Interrupt In.

Interface alignment.  This port was first written against an earlier
revision of the tree; every interface item below was re-verified
against this revision when the port was re-homed:

  interface alignment: vtable initialiser field set/names -> unchanged
  (verified).  common/usb_hc.h defines struct usbh_hc_driver with
  driver_name, driver_desc, init, deinit, get_frame_number,
  roothub_control, submit_urb, kill_urb and irq_handler at both
  revisions, and the initialiser fills exactly those slots.

  interface alignment: registration call site and name -> unchanged
  (verified).  The integrator calls usbh_register_hc_driver(busid,
  &xhci_hc_driver) before usbh_initialize(busid, reg_base,
  event_handler), which asserts the table is present
  (core/usbh_core.c); the port only exports the table.

  interface alignment: driver_name/driver_desc -> unchanged (verified):
  "xhci_hcd" / "xHCI Host Controller", the sibling EHCI table's shape.

  interface alignment: deinit slot semantics -> changed, and this is
  the only behaviour change against the previous revision.  At the
  earlier revision the vendored port/ehci/usb_hc_ehci.c supplied weak
  no-op usb_hc_low_level_init/usb_hc_low_level_deinit hooks, so a port
  that never called the deinit hook still linked.  At this revision
  both hooks are extern (integrators must implement them) and the EHCI
  sibling calls usb_hc_low_level_deinit() at the end of its
  usb_hc_deinit().  This port now declares the hook and calls it at
  the same point in its own deinit.

Integration surface: usb_hc_low_level_init()/usb_hc_low_level_deinit()
(the platform USB domain bring-up and teardown), usbh_console_printf()
(the console sink for the bring-up anchors; usb_config.h may point
CONFIG_USB_PRINTF at it), and the optional platform hooks declared in
the header (the post-start port power pass, the disconnected-event
slot release, the register/TD diagnostics dumps).  usb_config.h
sizes the port's pools through CONFIG_USBHOST_XHCI_EVENT_RING_SIZE,
_COMMAND_RING_SIZE, _TRANSFER_RING_SIZE, _MAX_SLOTS, _MAX_SPBUF and
_RINGS; CONFIG_USBHOST_MULT_HC selects the multi-controller renaming
that lets this port link next to the EHCI one.

Signed-off-by: zhugengyu <zhugengyu2023@gmail.com>
---
 port/xhci/usb_hc_xhci.c | 2687 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 port/xhci/usb_hc_xhci.h |  390 ++++++
 2 files changed, 3077 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 port/xhci/usb_hc_xhci.c
 create mode 100644 port/xhci/usb_hc_xhci.h

diff --git a/port/xhci/usb_hc_xhci.c b/port/xhci/usb_hc_xhci.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9223146
--- /dev/null
+++ b/port/xhci/usb_hc_xhci.c
@@ -0,0 +1,2687 @@
+/*
+ * @file   usb_hc_xhci.c
+ * @brief  DWC3 xHCI host controller driver for the RK3568 USB3 socket
+ *         group (CherryUSB usb_hc.h contract).
+ *
+ * The register sequence is NetBSD netbsd-11 sys/dev/usb/xhci.c (rev
+ * 1.188.2.4, BSD-2-Clause, notice below) - the implementation this
+ * driver descends from, board-proven on this SoC from a cold USB domain
+ * - crossed with the async URB model of the vendored
+ * port/ehci/usb_hc_ehci.c: IRQ-driven event ring processing, one URB in
+ * flight per endpoint with a per-ring waitsem for timeout'd submitters,
+ * completion callbacks from the interrupt path, the EHCI port's killed
+ * dance for the kill/complete race.  The lineage and every change
+ * against the previous revision of this port are recorded in the patch's
+ * commit message.
+ *
+ * Controller facts this file relies on (all board-measured):
+ *   - the DWC3 core needs the platform's CRU SRST pulse and the soft
+ *     reset closed with the GCTL.CORESOFTRESET clear, or the xHCI
+ *     aperture reads zero (usb_hc_low_level_init's sequence);
+ *   - this part reports CSZ=1 -> 64-byte contexts, spbuf=1 scratchpad;
+ *   - BSR=0 Address Device on this DWC3 makes the controller assign the
+ *     USB address itself - write slot address 0 and read the assigned
+ *     value from the output context, never the stack's dev_addr;
+ *   - PORTSC is W1C/RW mixed: ClearFeature writes only PP | the target
+ *     W1C bit (a stale PED snapshot written back disables the port).
+ *
+ * Cache discipline (non-coherent port, CONFIG_USB_DCACHE_ENABLE): rings,
+ * contexts and the ERST are cleaned after the CPU builds them; the event
+ * ring and output contexts are invalidated before every CPU read.
+ *
+ * HS/FS only - the SS lanes' combphys serve SATA on this SoC.
+ */
+/*	$NetBSD: xhci.c,v 1.188.2.4 $	*/
+/*-
+ * Copyright (c) 2013 The NetBSD Foundation, Inc.
+ * All rights reserved.
+ *
+ * This code is derived from software contributed to The NetBSD Foundation
+ * by Christos Zoulas and Nick Hudson.
+ *
